        <p>The Leap V2 is the predecessor to the Leap Plus and is available in certified refurbished condition for significantly less than any of the chairs above. Its adjustable lumbar system (height + firmness) and adjustable seat depth are the relevant features for lower back pain. The 20" seat height ceiling covers users through about 6'3" depending on proportions. For tall users who cannot justify $1,400–$1,600 for a Leap Plus, a certified refurbished Leap V2 from Crandall Office Furniture or similar dealers is the practical recommendation.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What Not to Do</h2>

        <h3>Adding a lumbar pillow to a chair that sits too low</h3>
        <p>Lumbar pillows and seat cushions are frequently recommended as solutions for office chair lower back pain. For tall users, they address neither root cause. A lumbar pillow on a chair that sits 2" too low still leaves you in posterior pelvic tilt — the pillow contacts your back but the pelvic geometry is wrong and the spine is still under anterior disc load. The ergonomic sequence is: get seat height right first, then address lumbar support. A pillow cannot substitute for correct seat height.</p>

        <h3>Buying a chair with adjustable lumbar but insufficient seat height</h3>
        <p>Many mid-range chairs advertise "adjustable lumbar support" but max out at 19"–20" seat height. For users at 6'3"+, this combination still leaves the pelvic tilt problem unsolved. The lumbar adjustment is useful but secondary — verify seat height ceiling first.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Why do tall people get more lower back pain from office chairs?</h3>
        <p>Two structural mismatches: lumbar support positioned too low (designed for users up to ~6'1", contacting mid-back of tall users instead of the lumbar curve) and seat height too low (tilting the pelvis posteriorly, flattening the lumbar curve). Both mechanisms create chronic spinal loading over long sessions. See the <a href="/why-standard-chairs-dont-fit/" class="link-internal">full explanation of why standard chairs don't fit tall people →</a></p>

        <h3>What is the best office chair for lower back pain for tall people?</h3>
        <p>The Steelcase Gesture (6'0"–6'3", tested at 6'4") and Steelcase Leap Plus (6'3"–6'7") are the most consistently recommended for tall users with lower back pain. Both provide adjustable seat depth (allowing full lumbar contact), adequate seat height ceilings, and dynamic or adjustable lumbar systems. Budget path: certified refurbished Steelcase Leap V2 (~$350–$500).</p>

        <h3>Does the Steelcase Gesture help with lower back pain?</h3>
        <p>For posture-driven lower back pain in tall users — yes, in my personal experience. The LiveBack backrest maintained lumbar contact through posture shifts in a way no previous chair I used did. The seat height range (up to 21") was also the first chair I owned that I didn't have to crank to maximum and still find too low. Pain resolved within a few weeks. Medical caveat: see the note at the top of this page about symptoms that warrant a physician visit rather than a chair change.</p>

        <h3>How high should the lumbar support be for a tall person?</h3>
        <p>Approximately 8"–12" above the seat pan, depending on height. At 6'0"–6'2", target 8"–10". At 6'3"–6'5", 10"–12". Standard chair lumbar systems reach 7"–9" — insufficient for most tall users. Prioritize chairs with lumbar height adjustment ranges that extend above 10", or dynamic backrest systems (like the Gesture's LiveBack) that automatically maintain correct contact through position changes.</p>
      </section>

